186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1045 "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059925 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "29cf2698-b761-565d-9655-9c36bff61aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $Q⇒(¬Q∧R).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "975bf3d1-7639-5d28-a9e4-e095a51c29d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060062 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\nQ⇒(¬Q∧R)≡¬Q∨(¬Q∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n¬Q∨(¬Q∧R)≡¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If you don’t study, then you won't pass the exam.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a21e616e-14f9-5690-b744-7b4501640855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060139 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't study.\n\n- $B:$ You won't pass the exam.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You study, or you won't pass the exam.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Study, or you won't pass the exam." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If you eat your vegetables, then you can have dessert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e854e89-958c-5853-be27-7985def8fc91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060140 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You eat your vegetables.\n\n- $B:$ You can have dessert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't eat your vegetables.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "866aa1bc-982d-58ab-8e07-dd5148fa5235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060143 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060552 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060552 #edb/ref 1061 139680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "082980d1-6f7a-54ee-8a3f-6ce0b731dc29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060553 #edb/ref 1061 139681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9889ed7d-e94e-5481-9500-3dcd8edb1f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060554 #edb/ref 1061 139684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1808ea7-1187-5229-8eb6-1d0bb6fff501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060555 #edb/ref 1061 139687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34a8f3e2-6b17-513b-bc4e-df73bc766ffa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060556 #edb/ref 1061 139690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f956015e-ca45-5f2f-8813-7e7f2a215cba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060984 #edb/ref 1064 "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$ \n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B\n$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as \n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.* \n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form. \n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:  \n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060984 #edb/ref 1065 7978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060984 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6634a172-a5c9-557f-9091-35a4ebd198d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060984 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378719 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "84fa8547-8670-5dc3-8bc0-1884fffcd80f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378720 #edb/ref 1080 "¬Q∧¬R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378720 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378720 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f7ae9dc-bc1c-5952-86bf-ac64d05dcdf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378721 #edb/ref 1080 "¬R∧Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378721 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378721 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27a7f796-45fe-50c0-b137-2daefb80bacb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378722 #edb/ref 1080 "¬Q∨¬R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9936400b-0feb-592e-a451-6d2689e03c68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378723 #edb/ref 1080 "¬Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378723 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378723 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5ed7cca-09a4-5119-9585-89d6dbbd28dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378724 #edb/ref 1080 "R∨¬Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17494fe9-de23-5826-8357-b89b12c9e401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬R⇒S).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f073aaca-ab2b-5cf0-b60f-e52fb1e200c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378732 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬R⇒S)≡¬(¬(¬R)∨S).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬R)∨S)≡¬(R∨S).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(R∨S)≡¬R∧¬S\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬R∧¬S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378736 #edb/ref 17592186